  "reason": "No experiments have been completed, so there is no evidence yet and maximal uncertainty across all design dimensions (modality, profile type, encoder, aggregation, fusion). The most informative first experiment is a simple, fast, interpretable baseline: RNA-only, single-profile, PCA-2000. This establishes (1) a performance floor for the pipeline, (2) the contribution of the RNA modality alone, and (3) a reference against which every subsequent experiment (adding image, switching to multiprofile, upgrading the encoder, or changing fusion) can be directly compared. Choosing the simplest encoder (PCA) also minimizes computational cost and reduces the risk of a failed run, preserving budget for more informative follow-ups. This is the best use of the first experiment because it maximally reduces uncertainty about the baseline and sets up clean ablations for all downstream design decisions."
}

